/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Set Matrix Zeroes
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Matrix
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given an m x n matrix, if an element is 0, set its entire
row and column to 0.

The modification must be performed in-place.

Approach:
Use the first row and first column of the matrix as marker
storage.

Whenever a zero is found at matrix[i][j], mark:

    matrix[i][0] = 0
    matrix[0][j] = 0

These markers tell us which rows and columns must eventually
be set to zero.

However, the first row and first column are also being used
as markers, so we separately store whether they originally
contained a zero.

After marking all rows and columns, update the inner matrix
using the stored markers. Finally, process the first row and
first column using the separate flags.

Why does this work?

The first row and first column provide enough space to store
the zero information without using an additional matrix.

We first record all required changes before modifying the
matrix values. This prevents newly created zeroes from being
mistaken for original zeroes.

The separate flags are necessary because the first row and
first column themselves are being used as marker storage.

Algorithm:
1. Check whether the first column originally contains zero.
2. Check whether the first row originally contains zero.
3. Use the first row and first column to mark zero rows/columns.
4. Update the inner matrix based on these markers.
5. If the first column originally contained zero, set it to zero.
6. If the first row originally contained zero, set it to zero.

Time Complexity : O(m × n)

Reason:
The matrix is traversed a constant number of times.

Space Complexity : O(1)

Reason:
The first row and first column are reused as marker storage,
so no additional arrays are required.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    void setZeroes(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {

        int rows = matrix.size();
        int columns = matrix[0].size();

        // Tracks whether the first column originally contained zero.
        bool firstColumnZero = false;

        // Tracks whether the first row originally contained zero.
        bool firstRowZero = false;

        // Check the first column.
        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            if (matrix[row][0] == 0) {
                firstColumnZero = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Check the first row.
        for (int column = 0; column < columns; column++) {
            if (matrix[0][column] == 0) {
                firstRowZero = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Use the first row and first column as markers.
        for (int row = 1; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int column = 1; column < columns; column++) {

                if (matrix[row][column] == 0) {
                    matrix[row][0] = 0;
                    matrix[0][column] = 0;
                }
            }
        }

        // Set marked rows and columns to zero.
        for (int row = 1; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int column = 1; column < columns; column++) {

                if (matrix[row][0] == 0 ||
                    matrix[0][column] == 0) {

                    matrix[row][column] = 0;
                }
            }
        }

        // Set the first column to zero if required.
        if (firstColumnZero) {
            for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
                matrix[row][0] = 0;
            }
        }

        // Set the first row to zero if required.
        if (firstRowZero) {
            for (int column = 0; column < columns; column++) {
                matrix[0][column] = 0;
            }
        }
    }
};
